Tue Oct 27 06:27:07 UTC 2026 - Michael Vetter <mvetter@suse.com>

- Update to 1.33:
  Changed behavior:
  * Accumulated values count from the start of each task. In -a and -A modes,
    iotop now shows the I/O that a task did since it started, as counted by the
    kernel, instead of only the I/O since iotop first saw it. A process that
    did its I/O before iotop was started is no longer shown as idle. With -A
    the average is over the age of the task. The total of a process includes
    its threads that iotop saw, but not the threads that exited before.
    --no-lifetime brings back the previous behavior, Ctrl-A toggles it and the
    setting is saved in the config file. (#84)
  * The IO and SWAPIN of a process are the longest that one of its threads
    waited in the cycle. They used to follow only the thread with the most
    delay since it started, so a process whose worker thread waited all the
    time could show 0 and sort as idle.
  * The interactive view refreshes when the delay is over. It used to wait in
    steps of 200 ms, so -d 0.5 refreshed every 0.6 s and any delay below 0.2 s
    became 0.2 s. A delay of 0 now refreshes as fast as the data can be read.
  * After an exec a process starts over as the new program that it shows, with
    a new graph and new totals since first seen.
  * Batch mode with -p reads only the process of the given task, with all its
    threads, instead of every task of the system. With many tasks a cycle took
    seconds before; now it takes almost nothing. The Total DISK READ and Total
    DISK WRITE then cover only that process.
  * The config file is replaced only when the new one is complete. -W and W
    write a temporary file next to it and rename it over the old one, so a
    crash or a failed write no longer leaves the config empty or cut. A
    symlinked config keeps its link, and the file keeps its mode and owner,
    also when root saves the config of a user. When that is not possible, e.g.
    in a directory that is not writable, the config is written in place as
    before. On a full disk the save now fails and keeps the old config.
  New features:
  * --flat lists the threads alone in the interactive view, sorted by their own
    values, instead of grouping them below their process. Ctrl-F toggles it,
    --no-flat turns it off and the setting is saved in the config file. It has
    no effect when only processes are shown. (#87)
  * --no-inverse turns off --inverse, e.g. after it was saved in the config file.
  * --debug-write works like -W, but writes the config with the preceding
    options to standard output instead of saving it, and exits. It is meant for
    debugging and testing. (Masatake YAMATO)
  * make check runs a test of the option parsing (Masatake YAMATO), without the config file of the user.
  * make scan runs the clang static analyzer (scan-build-23, or the one set in
    SCANBUILD) on a clean build. make pv also shows the compiler and the
    analyzer.
  * See https://github.com/Tomas-M/iotop/releases/tag/v1.33 for more

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Tue Oct 27 06:23:14 UTC 2026 - Michael Vetter <mvetter@suse.com>

- Update to 1.32:
  New features:
  * VFS READ and VFS WRITE columns show the bytes that each task reads and
    writes through the VFS layer, including I/O served from the page cache.
    They are hidden by default. Toggle them with v in interactive mode, or use
    --show-vfs/--hide-vfs and the per-column --show-vfs-read, --hide-vfs-read,
    --show-vfs-write and --hide-vfs-write. The setting is saved in the config
    file.
  * --uid2name=MODE sets how the USER column turns UIDs into user names:
    + always calls getpwuid(3) for every task;
    + none shows the numeric UID;
    + cyclic caches the names for one refresh cycle;
    + static, the default, caches them until Ctrl-L.
    UIDs without a name are cached too, and the mode is saved in the config file. (Masatake YAMATO)
  * --delay accepts fractional seconds, e.g. -d 0.5, always with . whatever the locale.
  * --time works as documented. --timestamp is accepted as a synonym, and with
    --time every batch line is prefixed with the time.
  * The interactive view shows the current units (IEC 1024 or SI 1000) in the top-right corner.
  * < and > scroll the help window when it does not fit on the screen.
  Performance:
  * Large systems with hundreds of thousands of tasks were the main target of
    this release. Each refresh now does much less work, and iotop uses less
    memory
  Fixes:
  * The I/O priority no longer shows the Linux 6.5+ priority hints as part of the level.
  * Taskstats replies are matched to their requests, so a late reply can no longer shift the data of the following tasks.
  * The netlink error code is no longer read from an incomplete reply.
  * The command name is taken from argv[0] only when the process has not rewritten its command line into one string (e.g. sshd: user@pts/0).
  * Graphs:
    + Process graphs are scaled by the aggregated history.
    + The graph maximum is taken from the visible rows only.
    + Fixed an out-of-bounds access in reversed Unicode graphs.
  * The first curses snapshot gets proper timestamps, so the first accumulated bandwidth values are right.
  * -n counts data refreshes in the curses view instead of loop iterations, and
    the last iteration is shown for a full delay.
  * Scrolling with PgUp/PgDn and the arrows starts from where the view is after the list has shrunk.
  * A use after free is fixed: the ionice selection pointed into a freed cycle.
  * A leak of the combining character buffer in the search input is fixed.
  * Config file:
    + Trailing whitespace, including the CR of CRLF line ends, is trimmed.
    + The directories are created only when saving.
    + An empty XDG_CONFIG_HOME is treated as unset, and the config is not placed in / when there is no home.
    + Truncated paths are not opened.
    + A failed save is reported, both with -W and with the W key.
  * Numeric option values (-n, -p, -u +UID, --threshold) are now validated.
    Invalid or out-of-range values are an error instead of being silently read
    as 0. Empty values for --help-type and -g are rejected.
  * getopt is fully reset before the command line is parsed after the config file.
  * In batch mode, a kernel whose taskstats version is too old now triggers a warning.
  * The code builds cleanly with many more compiler warnings enabled. Fixes for
    the clang static analyzer and NULL/PATH_MAX safety checks are included.
  * Documentation fixes in the README and the man page.
  What's Changed:
  * Add --user-decode option with pwuid, none, and cache methods #98
  * Add VFS READ and VFS WRITE columns to monitor logical I/O at syscall layer #100

